    "evidence": "Root cause / sink: util/src/main/java/io/kubernetes/client/Copy.java:140, inside Copy#copyDirectoryFromPod \u2014 `File f = new File(destination.toFile(), entry.getName());`. Trust boundary: `entry.getName()` originates from a TarArchiveEntry parsed out of the byte stream returned by `tar cz - <srcPath> | base64` executed *inside the remote pod/container* (Copy.java:123-130) \u2014 a compromised or malicious container is fully in control of these entry names, yet the client treats them as safe local filesystem path fragments. From this single unsanitized `f`, two distinct sinks are reachable and both must be fixed:\n  1. Copy.java:140/142 \u2014 directory-entry path: `if (!f.isDirectory() && !f.mkdirs())` creates attacker-chosen directories directly from the traversal-laden `f`.\n  2. Copy.java:140/146/147 \u2014 file-entry path: `File parent = f.getParentFile(); ... parent.mkdirs();` followed by `new FileOutputStream(f)` at line 150, creating attacker-chosen parent directories and writing attacker-controlled file content to an attacker-chosen path outside `destination`.\nBoth sinks were reproduced live: the PoV run shows `escaped file exists = true`, `escaped file outside dest = true`, `escaped file marker matches = true`, `escaped dir exists = true`, `escaped dir outside dest = true`, and exits 0. I additionally verified the negative control: adding a canonical-path containment check right after line 140 (throwing IOException when the resolved path escapes `destination`) causes the same PoV command to exit 1 (`copyDirectoryFromPod threw = true`, no escaped files/dirs), confirming the PoV is a faithful before/after gate for this vulnerability.",
