    "notes": "The alert contains two traces that share one root cause (Copy.java:140 `new File(destination.toFile(), entry.getName())`) fanning out to two sinks: trace 1 ends at line 142 (directory-entry mkdirs) and trace 2 ends at line 147 (file-entry parent.mkdirs(), followed by the write at line 150). I targeted the shared root cause directly and crafted the malicious tar payload to contain both a malicious directory entry and a malicious file entry in the same run, so the single PoV command exercises and verifies both sinks/traces in one shot (see the two escaped-file / escaped-dir assertions in the PoV output). I chose to exercise Copy.copyDirectoryFromPod(...) itself (rather than a synthetic unit test of a helper) by subclassing Copy and overriding only the inherited `exec(...)` transport call \u2014 the same technique the project's own CopyTest.java uses (there via WireMock) to avoid a live pod/websocket \u2014 so the tar-parsing and file/directory creation logic exercised is 100% real, unmodified production code. I validated the PoV's before/after gating by temporarily patching Copy.java with a canonical-path containment check and confirming the identical `run_pov.sh` command flips from exit 0 (vulnerable) to exit 1 (fixed), then reverted that temporary patch \u2014 the worktree's tracked files are unmodified; only `.security-pipeline/pov/` was added.",
    "pov_command": "bash .security-pipeline/pov/run_pov.sh",
    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/CopyTarSlipPov.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce: for every ArchiveEntry extracted in Copy#copyDirectoryFromPod, the canonical path of `new File(destination, entry.getName())` must be equal to, or a descendant of, the canonical path of `destination` \u2014 computed and checked *before* any filesystem mutation (mkdirs for directory entries at line 142, parent.mkdirs()+FileOutputStream for file entries at lines 146-150). Reject (or skip with a logged error) any entry that fails this check rather than silently rewriting/stripping the traversal segments, since a bespoke string-strip (e.g. only removing \"../\" prefixes) is easy to bypass. Known bypass variants the fix must also block, since they all reach the same `new File(destination, entry.getName())` sink:\n  - Nested/repeated traversal: \"a/../../evil.txt\", \"../../../../etc/cron.d/x\".\n  - Absolute path entry names: \"/etc/passwd\" or Windows-style \"C:\\\\evil.txt\" (`new File(dir, \"/etc/passwd\")` on many platforms yields \"/etc/passwd\", ignoring `dir` entirely).\n  - Symlink entries (TarArchiveEntry.isSymbolicLink()) whose *link target* points outside destination \u2014 a pure name-based containment check on the entry name does not prevent a benign-looking entry name from later being written through a previously-planted malicious symlink.\n  - Directory entries using the same traversal trick (must be checked identically to file entries \u2014 do not special-case the isDirectory() branch out of the containment check).\n  - Case/encoding variants on case-insensitive or Unicode-normalizing filesystems.\n  - Sibling call site with the identical unguarded pattern potential in copyFileToPod's inverse direction is out of scope here (that path writes a single explicit destPath, not entry-name-derived paths), but any other tar/zip extraction loop added to this client in the future should reuse a single shared, tested \"safe extract\" helper rather than re-implementing the `new File(dir, name)` pattern.",
